ds/github_task.py line 236 calls get_github() on BacklogBackend-typed issue_backend parameter | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius section documents this explicitly; line 224 `issue_backend: BacklogBackend` parameter; line 236 calls `self._issue_backend.get_github()` which will not exist on base BacklogBackend after split \u2014 this is a type error that must be resolved |\\n| 12 | Whether GitHubCapableBackend will be importable from sam_schema (cross-module export) | MISSING | Impact Radius identifies sam_schema as a consumer of the split Protocol; no documentation exists stating whether GitHubCapableBackend will be exported from backend_protocol.py for cross-module import, or whether sam_schema will re-import it |\\n| 13 | Type narrowing approach for call sites: isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend) or TypeGuard function | MISSING | Two options identified (Research section, existing TypeGuard pattern at operations.py line 504, artifact_provider.py line 83); no decision document states which approach operations.py will use. AC5 requires \\\"either (a) type-narrow to GitHubCapableBackend before call, or (b) runtime capability check\\\" \u2014 this choice affects all 37+ call sites |\\n| 14 | Current ty/basedpyright error baseline on affected call sites (before split) | DERIVABLE | Can be captured by running `uv run ty check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py 2>&1 \\\\| grep -c 'unresolved-attribute'` before any refactor code is written; this establishes the baseline for AC6 verification (zero errors after split) |\\n| 15 | Test coverage gap: AC3 negative conformance test for BeadsBackend vs GitHubCapableBackend does not exist | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius section; tests_backlog/ only test positive conformance; AC3 requires `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend) == False` which no test currently asserts |\\n| 16 | Type checker behavior: does ty correctly narrow after isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend) before calling sub-Protocol methods | AVAILABLE | Research section line 2308 \u2014 **VERIFIED**: ty correctly narrows after isinstance or TypeGuard; narrowing works as expected in Python 3.11.15 |\\n| 17 | Existing capability flag pattern (supports_batch_status_fetch, supports_batch_issue_update, issue_id_type) \u2014 is it established and used as an alternative to isinstance | AVAILABLE | Fact-checker VERIFIED: capability flags are actively used in operations.py lines 2428, 3611, 3945; Protocol documents these at backend_protocol.py lines 183\u2013193; established pattern that could serve as alternative to isinstance narrowing |\\n| 18 | InMemoryBackend and SQLiteBackend stubs for get_github/try_get_github \u2014 current state and disposition | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius section: InMemoryBackend has stubs at lines 124\u2013130; SQLiteBackend has stubs at lines 245\u2013251; both raise RuntimeError/return None similar to BeadsBackend. After split, these stubs must either be removed (option B) or retained and marker-method logic applied (option A) to prevent incorrect GitHubCapableBackend conformance |\\n| 19 | Documentation update scope: backend-providers.md, ARCHITECTURE.md, CLAUDE.md require method-group table updates | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius documentation section; backend-providers.md lines 104\u2013174 is the canonical reference (HIGH risk if stale); ARCHITECTURE.md lines 289\u2013314 is AI-agent-facing (MEDIUM risk); CLAUDE.md has summary-level references (LOW risk) |\\n| 20 | CI gates that must pass after refactor: typecheck-ty, typecheck-python (basedpyright), test-cross-backend \u2014 are all three blocking checks | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius CI section; code-quality.yml lines 59\u201371 (ty, required), lines 41\u201353 (basedpyright, required), lines 217\u2013234 (test-cross-backend matrix, required); AC6 explicitly requires zero type-check errors on these gates |\\n| 21 | Whether server.py direct get_github calls (lines 3990, 4159) are included in the 37 call-site count | AVAILABLE | Fact-checker verified 37 total occurrences; Research section impact measurement shows 2 direct calls in server.py are separate from 28 operations.py wrapper callers + 2 wrapper definitions = 32 total documented in Research, but fact-checker total is 37 (discrepancy explained: fact-checker grep counts all literal string matches; Research counted logical callstacks). Actual scope: all 37 must be covered |\\n| 22 | ADR-001 architect spec document \u2014 does it exist and contain the deferral decision | AVAILABLE | Fact-checker INCONCLUSIVE: ADR-001 is referenced in beads_backend.py module docstring but no standalone ADR or architect spec document exists; only ADR-1770-1 exists in docs/adrs/. The item description claims ADR-001 \\\"acknowledged and deferred\\\" this decision, but the claim cannot be verified without the primary source |\\n\\n**Counts**: AVAILABLE 15, DERIVABLE 2, MISSING 3\\n\\n**Verdict**: BLOCKED\\n\\n**Rationale**: Three conditions are MISSING and block planning:\\n\\n1. **Condition #10 (approach decision)** \u2014 The refactor cannot proceed without deciding which of three options (marker method, stub removal, TypeGuard) will satisfy AC3 (isinstance returns False for BeadsBackend). This decision affects the entire Protocol design, all backend implementations, and all narrowing logic. It is not derivable from code; it is an architectural choice that requires human decision.\\n\\n2. **Condition #12 (cross-module export)** \u2014 sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py has a type contract break at line 236 (calls get_github() on BacklogBackend-typed issue_backend). The refactor must decide whether GitHubCapableBackend will be exported from backend_protocol.py for cross-module import, or whether issue_backend parameter type must be changed to accept both BacklogBackend and GitHubCapableBackend. This decision affects the cross-module API contract and requires verification of whether sam_schema can import from backlog_core.\\n\\n3. **Condition #13 (type narrowing approach)** \u2014 The refactor must decide whether all 37 call sites will use `isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend)` narrowing or a `TypeGuard` function wrapping capability-flag checks. While both are technically viable (Research confirms ty handles both), AC5 leaves the choice open and no decision document exists. This blocks task decomposition because the approach affects whether operations.py wrappers are modified, whether a new TypeGuard helper is added, and how each call site is narrowed.\\n\\n**Unblock actions required**:\\n\\n1. **Decision: AC3 satisfaction approach** \u2014 Document which of options A (marker method), B (stub removal), or C (TypeGuard on flag) will be used. Impacts: BeadsBackend/InMemoryBackend/SQLiteBackend implementation strategy, Protocol design (whether GitHubCapableBackend has marker method), test negation assertions.\\n\\n2. **Decision: cross-module export strategy** \u2014 Confirm whether GitHubCapableBackend will be exported from backend_protocol for import by sam_schema, or whether issue_backend parameter type will be widened. Impacts: sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py type annotation update, backend_protocol exports.\\n\\n3. **Decision: call-site narrowing pattern** \u2014 Confirm whether all 37 call sites use `isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend)` narrowing (operations.py wrappers + server.py direct calls) or `TypeGuard` wrapping (consistency with existing codebase pattern). Impacts: operations.py wrapper signature change, whether new TypeGuard helper is added, operations.py call-site edits.\\n\\n**Planning dependency for these decisions**: These are bounded local decisions for which Safe Default Gateway criteria can be checked once design intent is clarified. No indefinite research is required \u2014 three options are known and viable. This is an ASK-USER gate that should be resolved in a brief synchronous clarification pass before task decomposition begins.\\n\\n**Changes from snapshot** (first-pass RT-ICA 