y covers `GitHubCapableBackend` conformance at all\\n  - scope: the refactor touches 8 Python modules plus 4 test files plus 3 docs; scope is larger than the item description implies (described as a Protocol definition change; actually requires 36 call-site narrowings, cross-module type annotation updates in `sam_schema`, and new negative conformance tests)\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - `sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py` \u2014 not mentioned in item description; discovered via grep; cross-module type incompatibility where `BacklogBackend`-typed `issue_backend` parameter calls `get_github()` which moves to sub-Protocol\\n  - `backlog_core/_branch_delegates.py` \u2014 integration branch dispatch wrappers; branch methods are in the GitHub-specific 24-method group; delegation wrappers will need narrowing\\n  - `backlog_core/backends/memory_backend.py` and `sqlite_backend.py` \u2014 both have `get_github`/`try_get_github` stubs and `assert isinstance(backend, BacklogBackend)` guards; must be handled carefully to avoid incorrectly satisfying `GitHubCapableBackend`\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved (AC2, AC3, AC4 negative conformance assertions not yet in any test)\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked (`sam_schema` cross-module dependency confirmed)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAlignment assessment: ALIGNED\\n\\n### Concerns\\n| Category | Proposed Change Excerpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n0 concerns identified \u2014 proposed change is consistent with product mission and historical direction.\\n\\n**Alignment rationale:**\\n\\n1. **Strengthens pluggable backend abstraction** \u2014 The development harness CLAUDE.md (line 3) establishes the core identity as a \\\"language-agnostic development process harness.\\\" Backend abstraction is the mechanism enabling that language-agnosticism. The proposed split to separate generic `BacklogBackend` from GitHub-specific `GitHubCapableBackend` directly reinforces this mission by enforcing cleaner boundaries between platform-agnostic and platform-specific operations.\\n\\n2. **Maintains composition model discipline** \u2014 CLAUDE.md Composition Model (lines 187\u2013215) and backend-providers.md (lines 104\u2013120) document that the harness owns \\\"pluggable backend abstraction\\\" with explicitly documented method groups divided between generic (CRUD, sync, serialization) and GitHub-specific (GraphQL mutations, integration branch management, Projects V2). The current monolithic Protocol violates this documented separation; splitting it restores the designed architecture.\\n\\n3. **Enables clean protocol conformance for non-GitHub backends** \u2014 backend-providers.md (lines 122\u2013130) lists four active backends: GitHub, SQLite, InMemory, and Beads. The BeadsBackend currently satisfies no Protocol boundary and masks failures via `__getattr__`. The proposed `GitHubCapableBackend` sub-Protocol allows BeadsBackend to implement only the generic `BacklogBackend` Protocol, making type-system guarantees explicit and testable via `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), BacklogBackend)`.\\n\\n4. **Addresses documented architectural debt** \u2014 Item description cites ADR-001: \\\"refactor to GitHubSpecificBackend sub-Protocol later to avoid auditing 30+ call sites.\\\" The proposal implements exactly that ADR decision. Historical direction is aligned: this refactor was explicitly deferred pending cost-benefit analysis and has now passed that gate (AI-parallelized execution makes 30 call-site changes tractable).\\n\\n5. **Follows type-system discipline established in the harness** \u2014 The harness uses Protocol-based abstraction throughout (BacklogBackend, TaskBackend, ArtifactBackend per CLAUDE.md and backend-providers.md). This proposal extends the established pattern: type-narrow at call sites before invoking capability-specific methods. The acceptance criteria (AC5: type narrowing OR runtime capability checks; AC6: ty exits 0 with no per-line suppressions) enforce this discipline at the type-checker level, consistent with the harness's commitment to observable correctness (CLAUDE.md \\\"Every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact\\\").\\n\\nNo contradictions with the harness mission, design principles, or historical direction identified.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-30T11:59:33.323740Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] `list_milestones`/`create_milestone`/`get_soonest_milestone` dispatch through the backend abstraction instead of calling `get_github()` unconditionally.\\n- [ ] `sqlite_backend.py`'s existing `milestones`/`item_milestones` schema is actually reachable through these operations (or superseded by an intentionally redesigned schema, if planning determines the current one doesn't fit the priority-ordered-todo-list use case).\\n- [ ] Beads' native `epic` entity is mapped onto (or explicitly documented as distinct from) this system's milestone concept, so beads-backed projects have a working equivalent rather than ADR-001's current \\\"not implemented, no stubs.\\\"\\n- [ ] `MilestoneInfo`'s `state` field (or its replacement) is no longer typed/documented as GitHub-GraphQL-specific once a second backend implements it for real.\\n- [ ] A defined mechanism exists for grooming, researching, and implementing every issue in a milestone/group together with cross-agent awareness of the whole group's changes and trade-offs across parallel worktrees \u2014 not just per-item grooming under a shared label.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **split backlogbackend protocol to separate github-specific methods from generic backlog operations** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Observed during Pdec8934d \u2014 backlog_groom and all MCP backlog tools failed with \\\"try_get_github attribute missing\\\" because BacklogBackend Protocol requires GitHub-specific methods that BeadsBackend cannot implement. ADR-001 in architect spec deferred this citing 30+ call sites; user confirmed that cost is negligible for AI-parallelized execution.\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-15\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Observed during Pdec8934d \u2014 backlog_groom and all MCP backlog tools failed with \\\"try_get_github attribute missing\\\" because BacklogBackend Protocol requires GitHub-specific methods that BeadsBackend cannot implement. ADR-001 in architect spec deferred this citing 30+ call sites; user confirmed that cost is negligible for AI-parallelized execution.\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-15\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nAlignment assessment: ALIGNED\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n0 concerns identified \u2014 proposed change is consistent with product mission and historical direction.\\n\\n**Alignment rationale:**\\n\\n1. **Strengthens pluggable backend abstraction** \u2014 The development harness CLAUDE.md (line 3) establishes the core identity as a \\\"language-agnostic development process harness.\\\" Backend abstraction is the mechanism enabling that language-agnosticism. The proposed split to separate generic `BacklogBackend` from GitHub-specific `GitHubCapableBackend` directly reinforces this mission by enforcing cleaner boundaries between platform-agnostic and platform-specific operations.\\n\\n2. **Maintains composition model discipline** \u2014 CLAUDE.md Composition Model (lines 187\u2013215) and backend-providers.md (lines 104\u2013120) document that the harness owns \\\"pluggable backend abstraction\\\" with explicitly documented method groups divided between generic (CRUD, sync, serialization) and GitHub-specific (GraphQL mutations, integration branch management, Projects V2). The current monolithic Protocol violates this documented separation; splitting it restores the designed architecture.\\n\\n3. **Enables clean protocol conformance for non-GitHub backends** \u2014 backend-providers.md (lines 122\u2013130) lists four active backends: GitHub, SQLite, InMemory, and Beads. The BeadsBackend currently satisfies no Protocol boundary and masks failures via `__getattr__`. The proposed `GitHubCapableBackend` sub-Protocol allows BeadsBackend to implement only the generic `BacklogBackend` Protocol, making type-system guarantees explicit and testable via `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), BacklogBackend)`.\\n\\n4. **Addresses documented architectural debt** \u2014 Item description cites ADR-001: \\\"refactor to GitHubSpecificBackend sub-Protocol later to avoid auditing 30+ call sites.\\\" The proposal implements exactly that ADR decision. Historical direction is aligned: this refactor was explicitly deferred pending cost-benefit analysis and has now passed that gate (AI-parallelized execution makes 30 call-site changes tractable).\\n\\n5. **Follows type-system discipline established in the harness** \u2014 The harness uses Protocol-based abstraction throughout (BacklogBackend, TaskBackend, ArtifactBackend per CLAUDE.md and backend-providers.md). This proposal extends the established pattern: type-narrow at call sites before invoking capability-specific methods. The acceptance criteria (AC5: type narrowing OR runtime capability checks; AC6: ty exits 0 with no per-line suppressions) enforce this discipline at the type-checker level, consistent with the harness's commitment to observable correctness (CLAUDE.md \\\"Every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact\\\").\\n\\nNo contradictions with the harness mission, design principles, or historical direction identified.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-2</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **split backlogbackend protocol to separate github-specific methods from generic backlog operations** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:31:52.945386Z-0</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n\\n**Rationale**: This is a defect \u2014 a design flaw in the Protocol structure that allows callers to invoke GitHub-specific methods on non-GitHub backends at runtime with no type-system warning. The cause chain is traceable: (1) Protocol mixes generic + GitHub-specific methods, (2) BeadsBackend implements the full Protocol despite having no GitHub integration, (3) operations.py calls GitHub methods directly without type discrimination, (4) runtime NotImplementedError results with no compile-time signal. The system is broken by design and can be reproduced.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys via /find-cause skill \u2014 evidence chain from Protocol structure through BeadsBackend implementation to operations.py call sites.\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Current \u2014 callers invoke GitHub-specific methods on BeadsBackend and receive runtime NotImplementedError with no type-system warning; impossible to distinguish unsupported operation from operation failure. Improved \u2014 Protocol split into generic BacklogBackend + GitHubCapableBackend sub-Protocol; BeadsBackend implements only generic Backend; operations.py narrows type to GitHubCapableBackend before invoking GitHub methods; mypy/ty catches call-site errors at compile time.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:31:52.945386Z-1</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n\\n**Rationale**: This is a defect \u2014 a design flaw in the Protocol structure that allows callers to invoke GitHub-specific methods on non-GitHub backends at runtime with no type-system warning. The cause chain is traceable: (1) Protocol mixes generic + GitHub-specific methods, (2) BeadsBackend implements the full Protocol despite having no GitHub integration, (3) operations.py calls GitHub methods directly without type discrimination, (4) runtime NotImplementedError results with no compile-time signal. The system is broken by design and can be reproduced.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys via /find-cause skill \u2014 evidence chain from Protocol structure through BeadsBackend implementation to operations.py call sites.\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Current \u2014 callers invoke GitHub-specific methods on BeadsBackend and receive runtime NotImplementedError with no type-system warning; impossible to distinguish unsupported operation from operation failure. Improved \u2014 Protocol split into generic BacklogBackend + GitHubCapableBackend sub-Protocol; BeadsBackend implements only generic Backend; operations.py narrows type to GitHubCapableBackend before invoking GitHub methods; mypy/ty catches call-site errors at compile time.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:32:15.685833Z-0</sub>\\n\\n**Method**: 5-whys via /find-cause skill\\n\\n**Evidence chain**:\\n1. Observed failure: Callers cannot distinguish \\\"operation unsupported by this backend\\\" from \\\"operation failed\\\" when invoking GitHub-specific methods on non-GitHub backends like BeadsBackend.\\n2. Why: BeadsBackend implements the full BacklogBackend Protocol even though it has no GitHub integration; GitHub-specific methods raise NotImplementedError.\\n3. Why: The Protocol design interleaves generic backlog operations (CRUD, status mutations, serialization) with GitHub-specific methods (GraphQL, integration branches, Projects V2) in a single interface definition.\\n4. Why: All backend implementations must conform to the same Protocol signature. No type-system way to express \\\"this backend supports GitHub\\\" or \\\"this backend does not support GitHub.\\\"\\n5. Why: Protocol design allows operations.py to call GitHub-specific methods directly on a generic BacklogBackend type without any compile-time type check or runtime type guard.\\n\\n**Root cause**: BacklogBackend Protocol mixes generic and GitHub-specific methods in a single interface (`backend_protocol.py:156-193`). This design choice forces all backends to claim conformance to the full Protocol, even when they lack GitHub integration. Call sites in `operations.py` (lines 115, 124, 135, 150, 159, 179, 198, 207, 216) invoke GitHub methods directly on the generic `BacklogBackend` type without type discrimination, so calling these methods on BeadsBackend produces silent NotImplementedError at runtime.\\n\\n**Fix locus**: Split `backend_protocol.py` BacklogBackend Protocol into two: a generic `BacklogBackend` (CRUD, status, serialization) and a `GitHubCapableBackend` sub-Protocol extending it (GraphQL, integration branches, Projects V2 operations). Update `BeadsBackend` to implement only `BacklogBackend`. Narrow type of `repository` in operations.py call sites to `GitHubCapableBackend` before invoking GitHub methods (34 locations identified in Impact section).\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:32:15.685833Z-1</sub>\\n\\n**Method**: 5-whys via /find-cause skill\\n\\n**Evidence chain**:\\n1. Observed failure: Callers cannot distinguish \\\"operation unsupported by this backend\\\" from \\\"operation failed\\\" when invoking GitHub-specific methods on non-GitHub backends like BeadsBackend.\\n2. Why: BeadsBackend implements the full BacklogBackend Protocol even though it has no GitHub integration; GitHub-specific methods raise NotImplementedError.\\n3. Why: The Protocol design interleaves generic backlog operations (CRUD, status mutations, serialization) with GitHub-specific methods (GraphQL, integration branches, Projects V2) in a single interface definition.\\n4. Why: All backend implementations must conform to the same Protocol signature. No type-system way to express \\\"this backend supports GitHub\\\" or \\\"this backend does not support GitHub.\\\"\\n5. Why: Protocol design allows operations.py to call GitHub-specific methods directly on a generic BacklogBackend type without any compile-time type check or runtime type guard.\\n\\n**Root cause**: BacklogBackend Protocol mixes generic and GitHub-specific methods in a single interface (`backend_protocol.py:156-193`). This design choice forces all backends to claim conformance to the full Protocol, even when they lack GitHub integration. Call sites in `operations.py` (lines 115, 124, 135, 150, 159, 179, 198, 207, 216) invoke GitHub methods directly on the generic `BacklogBackend` type without type discrimination, so calling these methods on BeadsBackend produces silent NotImplementedError at runtime.\\n\\n**Fix locus**: Split `backend_protocol.py` BacklogBackend Protocol into two: a generic `BacklogBackend` (CRUD, status, serialization) and a `GitHubCapableBackend` sub-Protocol extending it (GraphQL, integration branches, Projects V2 operations). Update `BeadsBackend` to implement only `BacklogBackend`. Narrow type of `repository` in operations.py call sites to `GitHubCapableBackend` before invoking GitHub methods (34 locations identified in Impact section).\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:36:47.535410Z-0</sub>\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backend-providers.md` \u2014 contains the canonical `BacklogBackend` Protocol documentation section (lines 104\u2013174) listing all method groups including GitHub-specific ones as part of the single Protocol; this section describes the current single-Protocol design and will be entirely stale after the split; method group table must be updated to show `BacklogBackend` (generic) vs `GitHubCapableBackend` (GitHub-specific); capability comparison table must be updated | Risk: HIGH | Why: primary human-facing documentation for backend implementors; a developer adding a new backend (GitLab, Linear, Supabase) reads this document; stale content teaches the old pattern and would cause new backends to implement the old monolithic Protocol, defeating the purpose of the refactor\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` \u2014 describes `BacklogBackend` Protocol at lines 289\u2013314; method groups listed at line 295 includes GitHub-specific groups as part of a single Protocol; module table at lines 41\u201342 describes `backends/` as implementing `BacklogBackend Protocol` \u2014 must be updated to reflect the two-Protocol design | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: AI-agent-facing architecture document; agents reading this for context will reason incorrectly about the Protocol surface\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 references `BacklogBackend` Protocol at lines 521, 605; brief references that describe the Protocol as a single entity; will become stale but the references are summary-level, not method-level | Risk: LOW | Why: high-level summary reference; less likely to mislead implementors than backend-providers.md; still needs a one-line update mentioning the split\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-lifecycle.md` \u2014 references `BacklogBackend` Protocol at line 217 and links to `backend-providers.md` at line 506; will remain accurate if `backend-providers.md` is updated; the reference itself is accurate at a high level | Risk: LOW | Why: indirect reference; link to backend-providers.md is the load-bearing claim; updating backend-providers.md is sufficient\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md` \u2014 references `get_github`, `try_get_github`, and the public function surface; specifically FIND-20 mentions these; after the split the audit notes about private vs public naming may need updating; the audit tracks `_get_github()` \u2192 `get_github()` renaming history which is Protocol-adjacent | Risk: LOW | Why: documentation audit file, not a runtime file; informational drift risk\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/multi-perspective-review/references/acceptance-test-guide.md` \u2014 references `backend_protocol.py` at line 82 in the context of test file classification; the reference is about what NOT to classify as UI tests; will remain accurate after the split | Risk: LOW | Why: reference is to the file path, not the Protocol content; file path is unchanged\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved (AC2, AC3, AC4 negative conformance assertions not yet in any test)\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked (`sam_schema` cross-module dependency confirmed)\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:36:47.535410Z-1</sub>\\n\\n### Ecosystem and Compatibility\\n\\n**Python 3.11:** Supported \u2014 runtime behavior verified.\\n\\n**typing_extensions Protocol:** Supported \u2014 confirmed as the base class used in `backlog_core` (`from typing_extensions import Protocol`).\\n\\n**ty (Astral) 0.0.28:** [CROSS-ANGLE] Supported \u2014 verified via `ty check` on test files. ty correctly:\\n- Catches calling sub-Protocol methods on a base Protocol type without narrowing (`error[unresolved-attribute]`)\\n- Passes when `isinstance()` narrowing precedes the sub-Protocol method call\\n- Passes when `TypeGuard` narrowing precedes the call\\n- Does NOT flag `b: GitHubCapableBackend = BeadsBackend()` as an error \u2014 because BeadsBackend structurally satisfies `GitHubCapableBackend` (has the method names). This is correct structural-typing behavior, not a ty bug.\\nSOURCE: live `ty check` on `/tmp/test_protocol_ty*.py`, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n**[CROSS-ANGLE] CRITICAL FINDING \u2014 AC cannot be satisfied by structural isinstance alone:**\\n\\n`isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend)` returns **True** at runtime \u2014 even if `GitHubCapableBackend` is a sub-Protocol that adds `get_github` and `try_get_github`. This is because BeadsBackend already HAS those method names as stubs (`beads_backend.py` lines 169\u2013176):\\n\\n- `get_github`: raises `NotImplementedError(_ADR_001_NOTE)` (line 170)\\n- `try_get_github`: returns `None` (line 173)\\n\\nSince `@runtime_checkable` isinstance checks only for name presence, the structural sub-Protocol pattern alone cannot produce `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend) == False`. The Acceptance Criteria for #2287 require a non-structural discriminator.\\n\\nSOURCE: beads_backend.py lines 169\u2013176; live runtime test confirmed `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend) == True`, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n**Verified workarounds (all tested live):**\\n\\n1. **Marker method approach (recommended):** Add a unique sentinel method to `GitHubCapableBackend` that is NOT present in `BeadsBackend` \u2014 e.g., `_github_capability_marker(self) -> None`. Confirmed: `isinstance(BeadsLike(), GitHubCapableBackend) == False` when the marker is absent from `BeadsLike`. SOURCE: live runtime test, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n2. **Remove method stubs from BeadsBackend:** If `get_github` and `try_get_github` are removed from `BeadsBackend` entirely, `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend)` returns `False`. SOURCE: live runtime test, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n3. **TypeGuard checking a flag value:** A `TypeGuard` function checking `getattr(backend, '_is_github_capable', False)` (attribute VALUE not name presence) avoids the structural isinstance limitation entirely and is consistent with the existing capability-flag pattern. SOURCE: live runtime test, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Gaps (cross-angle)\\n\\n- External community sources (CPython issue tracker, GitHub discussions, PyPI bug reports) not searched by either the API State or Ecosystem Research angles. If known bugs in sub-Protocol `isinstance()` behavior exist beyond the Python 3.12.0 regression, they may not be captured here. Priority: Low \u2014 the live runtime verification on Python 3.11.15 directly confirms behavior.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Gaps (single-angle)\\n\\n- API State: typing_extensions changelog not fetched (no `__version__` attribute in installed version; exact version unknown).\\n- Impact Measurement: No runtime performance benchmark for `isinstance()` on `@runtime_checkable` Protocol in Python 3.11 specifically.\\n- Impact Measurement: No benchmark comparing isinstance-guard overhead vs capability-flag check overhead for the 32 call sites.\\n- Codebase Auditor: BeadsBackend test suite (`backlog_core/tests/`) not scanned for existing isinstance tests against `BeadsBackend` \u2014 may have existing tests that encode current behavior expectations.\\n- Codebase Auditor: `GitHubBackend` and `SQLiteBackend` implementations not examined to confirm they also have `get_github`/`try_get_github` (and whether they would satisfy `GitHubCapableBackend` structurally \u2014 important for the marker method approach).\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-31T09:51:02.142512Z</sub>\\n\\n**Scope expanded 2026-08-31** (discovered during a docstring-truth audit of `backlog_core/server.py` and an adversarial-solution-design concept review requested in the same session, folded in per user decision rather than filed as a separate item \u2014 see #3275 for the related precedent on backend-name leakage into a supposedly backend-agnostic tool surface, that one for a parameter name, this one for exception typing).\\n\\n**The `isinstance(backend, GitHubExtras)` capability gate is broken for 2 of 4 backends, not just inconsistently worded.** Verified live: `SQLiteBackend` and `InMemoryBackend` both satisfy `GitHubExtras` (a 19-method `@runtime_checkable` Protocol) *structurally* \u2014 they define all 19 methods as `NotImplementedError` stubs, and `runtime_checkable` `isinstance` only checks attribute presence, not real implementation. Result: at all 17 call sites that gate GitHub-only functionality this way (`operations.py` lines 137, 188, 315, 327, 339, 351, 368, 383, 395, 409, 421, 435, 447, 459, 471; `server.py` lines 3614, 3776), SQLite and Memory sail *past* the guard and hit a raw, uncaught `NotImplementedError` instead of the intended `BacklogError` \u2014 only Beads is ever actually caught. `backend_types.py:222-224` sanctions exactly the stub pattern that defeats its own gate.\\n\\n**Five different dialects exist for \\\"backend lacks this capability,\\\" not two:** (1) `BacklogError(\\\"<fn> requires a GitHub-backed backend\\\")` \u2014 the 17 sites above; (2) `UnsupportedCapabilityError(ContentProviderError)` \u2014 `github_content_stores.py:160,235`, `github_content_migration.py:336,380`; (3) `RuntimeError`/`TypeError` from `_branch_delegates.py:39,42`'s `_require_branch_support`; (4) a returned (not raised) `{\\\"error\\\": \\\"...requires a GitHub-backed backend\\\"}` dict from `dh_core/operations.py:1245,1358` \u2014 notably the *same two tools* as two of the 17 sites (`dispatch_stale_check`, `dispatch_conflicts`), implemented twice with identical prose, one raising and one returning; (5) bare `NotImplementedError` from backend stubs, sanctioned by `backend_types.py:224`.\\n\\n**The pattern the user wants already exists and is validated** \u2014 `models.py:602-609`: `BackendUnavailableError(BacklogError)` / `GitHubUnavailableError(BackendUnavailableError)`, docstring: \\\"Callers catching this exception handle any backend absence uniformly; callers that need to distinguish backend types... catch the appropriate subclass.\\\" Capability gaps should mirror this shape rather than invent a sixth dialect. Separately, `_branch_delegates.py:34` already checks a `supports_branches` flag *first*, `isinstance` only as a secondary assertion \u2014 that ordering is the fix the 17 `GitHubExtras` sites are missing, and is the correct template to copy.\\n\\n`UnsupportedCapabilityError` is *not* a drop-in fix for the 17 sites: it's rooted under `ContentProviderError(Exception)`, a separate tree from `BacklogError(Exception)`. `tests_backlog/test_artifact_provider.py:1181-1185` documents that split as intentional contract (\\\"ContentNotFoundError is a ContentProviderError, not a BacklogError, so it is not caught... This test documents the existing behaviour as a specification test\\\"). Reusing it as-is for the 17 sites would silently escape roughly 50 `except BacklogError` handlers in `server.py`. It also carries prose only, no structured fields \u2014 unlike its siblings in the same file (`ItemNotFoundError.selector`, `EntryNotFoundError.entry_id/available`, `BranchConflictError.head_branch/base_branch/conflict_files`, etc.), which all carry structured data.\\n\\n**Recommended direction** (from the adversarial-solution-design review, not yet implemented): add a `supports_github_extras: bool` flag to `WorkItemBackend` alongside the existing three capability flags, gate all 17+2 sites flag-first (mirroring `_require_branch_support`), and raise one new `UnsupportedBackendCapabilityError(BacklogError)` carrying `capability`/`backend`/`operation` fields \u2014 closes the actual SQLite/Memory false-negative, stays in the `BacklogError` tree (zero handler churn across the ~50 existing catch sites), and also fold `_require_branch_support`'s ad-hoc `RuntimeError`/`TypeError` into the same helper so every optional-protocol gate behaves identically. Separately, fix the two MCP-boundary tools (`dispatch_stale_check`, `dispatch_conflicts`) to return a structured `{\\\"error\\\": ..., \\\"unsupported_capability\\\": ..., \\\"backend\\\": ...}` dict instead of raising, deduplicating them against their `dh_core/operations.py` twins. Explicitly deferred: unifying the `ContentProviderError`/`BacklogError` trees \u2014 genuine debt, but load-bearing (documented as contract in the spec test above), touches every `except` tuple in `server.py`, and buys nothing beyond the flag+subclass fix above; if pursued, it needs its own change and its own review, not a rider here.\\n\\nOne correctness bug worth fixing regardless of which direction wins: `server.py:3629`/`:3795` catch `(BacklogError, _GithubException)` and relabel *any* `BacklogError` \u2014 including a capability gap \u2014 as `\\\"GitHub API error: {exc}\\\"`, which is false for a capability gap and contradicts those same tools' own docstrings (\\\"error on file/parse or GitHub failure\\\").\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-31T23:53:44.436366Z</sub>\\n\\n**Correction 2026-09-01** (verified against current code before starting slice 1 \u2014 the capability-gate fix): the Description and the 2026-08-31 Context entry both describe a codebase state that no longer matches `main`.\\n\\n1. **`BacklogBackend` no longer exists.** `backend_types.py:111-113` carries an explicit tombstone: \\\"The monolithic `BacklogBackend` Protocol that previously bundled all 46 methods has been removed; call sites use `WorkItemBackend` (and `GitHubExtras` / `BranchBackend` where appropriate).\\\" The Protocol split this item's Description asks for already shipped, as five layered protocols (`WorkItemBackend`, `SyncProvider`, `ContentProvider`, `GitHubExtras`, `BranchBackend`, all `@runtime_checkable`). Acceptance criteria items covering the protocol split are already satisfied; the milestone- and exception-related criteria are not.\\n2. **SQLite and Memory do not stub `GitHubExtras` with `NotImplementedError`.** They implement all 19 methods with working local simulations (SQLite tables, in-memory dicts) \u2014 including a working `_fetch_milestones_graphql` at `sqlite_backend.py:1027` backed by a real `milestones`/`item_milestones` schema. Only `_update_issues_graphql_batch` raises on either backend.\\n3. **The actual failure mode is a bare `RuntimeError`, not `NotImplementedError`.** Traced `list_milestones` end to end: `operations.py:4499` calls `get_github(repo)` \u2192 the delegator at `operations.py:130` runs `isinstance(backend, GitHubExtras)`, which SQLite passes \u2192 `sqlite_backend.py:424` raises `RuntimeError(\\\"SQLiteBackend.get_github: no GitHub connection available\\\")`. The call dies before ever reaching SQLite's working milestone table.\\n4. **There are nine dialects for \\\"backend lacks capability,\\\" not five.** The 2026-08-31 entry's five-dialect inventory missed the most consequential one: roughly 13 `isinstance(backend, SyncProvider)` sites (e.g. `operations.py:1664-1666`, `:3207-3208`, `:3925-3927`, `:3998-3999`, `:4065-4066`, `server.py:1195-1200`) that log an info line and return a **zeroed success-shaped dict** with no exception at all \u2014 the caller cannot distinguish \\\"nothing to sync\\\" from \\\"this backend cannot sync.\\\" It also missed silent `None`/`{}`/`[]`/no-op returns (`sqlite_backend.py:428,442`; `memory_backend.py:238,253,412`; `beads_backend.py:430,610,751-780`) and one site that silently drops four of its caller's arguments (`beads_backend.py:549-551`).\\n\\nFull nine-dialect inventory and file:line citations are preserved for the follow-up items (slices B/C/D) rather than repeated here. This entry does not change the Description or Acceptance Criteria \u2014 it only corrects stale factual claims so planning starts from the real code state.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"7142df1d0b25d692e6483476020691fee4881f29bfcf617b5c2af173865d28b0\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABR4NDVQ\"}"}